# Beetroot micro-VM guest kernel config fragment (binderless-hosts QEMU/TCG).
#
# Apply on top of `make defconfig` (x86_64). Every option is built in (=y),
# never =m, because the guest rootfs ships no kernel modules. This is the
# §4.1 delta from docs/design/binderless-hosts-qemu-tcg.md — the exact set
# proven to boot redroid (Android 11+) to sys.boot_completed=1 under TCG.

# Binder (the whole point — the host has none, the guest brings its own).
# NB: there is no `CONFIG_ANDROID` symbol on modern kernels (the umbrella
# config was dropped; drivers/android/Kconfig now opens straight on
# `menu "Android"`). Validated absent on the pinned 6.12.9 build, where
# requesting it is a silent no-op. ANDROID_BINDER_IPC stands on its own.
CONFIG_ANDROID_BINDER_IPC=y
CONFIG_ANDROID_BINDERFS=y
CONFIG_ANDROID_BINDER_DEVICES="binder,hwbinder,vndbinder"

# Container runtime (Docker / containerd / runc run inside the guest).
CONFIG_NAMESPACES=y
CONFIG_USER_NS=y
CONFIG_PID_NS=y
CONFIG_NET_NS=y
CONFIG_CGROUPS=y
CONFIG_MEMCG=y
CONFIG_BLK_CGROUP=y
CONFIG_CPUSETS=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_PIDS=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_DEVICE=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_BPF=y
CONFIG_OVERLAY_FS=y
CONFIG_EXT4_FS=y
CONFIG_BRIDGE=y
CONFIG_VETH=y
CONFIG_TUN=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER=y
CONFIG_NF_NAT=y
CONFIG_IP_NF_IPTABLES=y
CONFIG_IP_NF_NAT=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_MASQUERADE=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_MATCH_ADDRTYPE=y

# bpf() syscall — REQUIRED: cgroup-v2 device control is eBPF-only. Without
# this, runc dies: bpf_prog_query(BPF_CGROUP_DEVICE) returns ENOSYS.
CONFIG_BPF_SYSCALL=y
CONFIG_BPF_JIT=y

# Guest I/O (virtio devices QEMU exposes; initrd + devtmpfs for boot).
CONFIG_VIRTIO_BLK=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_NET=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_PCI=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_CONSOLE=y
CONFIG_DEVTMPFS=y
CONFIG_DEVTMPFS_MOUNT=y
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_INITRD=y

# memfd_create — Android 11+ uses it in place of the removed ashmem driver.
CONFIG_MEMFD_CREATE=y

# Pressure-stall info — REQUIRED for lmkd stability. Without it lmkd
# crash-loops on /proc/pressure/memory and can eventually trigger an
# Android reboot. With it, a 5-minute idle stays clean (POC: STABLE_IDLE_OK).
CONFIG_PSI=y

# --- Trim: drop physical-hardware drivers a virtio guest can never bind ---
#
# `make defconfig` is the full desktop/server x86_64 kernel; the bulk of the
# ~3000-object compile (see benchmarks/) is hardware drivers for buses and
# cards that simply do not exist in our QEMU machine. Our launch line
# (`build_qemu_argv`) is `-M q35 -nographic -display none` with only
# virtio-blk, virtio-net, and a serial console — no real GPU, NIC, SATA, or
# SCSI HBA is ever presented, so these drivers can never probe a device.
# Disabling them is a pure compile-time / image-size win with no runtime
# behaviour change, validated by booting redroid to sys.boot_completed=1 and
# taking a screencap after the trim.
#
# Deliberately NOT trimmed (forward-looking, Beetroot is also a dev target):
#   * SOUND stays fully on — an app developer's audio code must run, and the
#     subsystem is cheap. Audio in redroid is a userspace HAL today, but we
#     keep the kernel side intact in case a virtio-snd device is wired up.
#   * DRM core + DRM_VIRTIO_GPU stay on — screenshots (`screencap`) work via
#     software composition today, and keeping virtio-gpu leaves the door open
#     to a real virtual display. Only the *physical* GPU driver (i915) is cut.
#   * The generic graphics/IPC infra Android userspace relies on
#     (DMA_SHARED_BUFFER, SYNC_FILE, MEMFD_CREATE) is core, not a hardware
#     driver, and is untouched.
#   * MEDIA_SUPPORT / WWAN are already off in defconfig (redroid's camera is a
#     userspace HAL); left as-is, not newly disabled.
#
# DRM_I915 — Intel GPU, the single largest driver in the tree. No i915 device
# on q35+virtio; redroid renders in software (swiftshader).
# CONFIG_DRM_I915 is not set
# ETHERNET — every vendor NIC driver. The guest's only NIC is virtio-net
# (CONFIG_VIRTIO_NET, kept above), which is not gated by this symbol.
# CONFIG_ETHERNET is not set
# WLAN — physical Wi-Fi hardware drivers. The wireless *stack* (cfg80211/
# mac80211) is separate; this only drops drivers for cards we don't have.
# CONFIG_WLAN is not set
# ATA — SATA/PATA host controllers. The guest root disk is virtio-blk
# (CONFIG_VIRTIO_BLK, kept above); no ATA controller is presented.
# CONFIG_ATA is not set
# SCSI low-level HBA drivers (megaraid, mpt3sas, qla, …). SCSI core stays on;
# only the drivers for physical HBAs we never expose are dropped.
# CONFIG_SCSI_LOWLEVEL is not set

# --- Build/image levers (no functional change for this guest) ---
#
# NB on -Os: CONFIG_CC_OPTIMIZE_FOR_SIZE was tried and reverted. It shrank the
# bzImage (~12→10 MiB) but ~DOUBLED the redroid boot under TCG (guest-measured
# 98→201 s) — the boot is CPU-bound ART/Zygote work and -Os trades run-time
# speed for size, which software emulation punishes hard. A 2 MiB saving is not
# worth a 2x slower boot, so we stay on the -O2 default
# (CONFIG_CC_OPTIMIZE_FOR_PERFORMANCE). The real compile-speed win is ccache
# (handled in build_vm_kernel), not -Os. See benchmarks/README.md.
#
# No loadable modules — the guest rootfs ships none, and `make bzImage` only
# builds the =y core anyway. Dropping module support removes the module
# infrastructure (kallsyms-for-modules, EXPORT_SYMBOL tables, modversions) for
# a small build + image win, and matches reality: everything the guest needs
# is built in. Any defconfig driver that was =m simply becomes =n (it was
# never loadable in a module-less guest regardless).
# CONFIG_MODULES is not set
